2774: \begin{proposition}[Current-package acceptance and certificate ledger]
2775: \label{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}
2776: Let $P^{+}$ be the finite current package consisting of the primary source
2777: \path{submission_abstract.tex}, this supplement \path{main.tex}, and, under the
2778: exact-or-archive-equivalent support-surface condition of
2779: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition},
2780: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, together with
2781: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
2782: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
2783: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
2784: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json},
2785: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py},
2786: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}, and the explicit
2787: historical paths named in the case catalogue.  Root-level diagnostics such as
2788: \path{PIPELINE.md}, \path{SOURCE_MAP.md}, \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}, and
2789: \path{source_interface_record.json} may explain local provenance or path-verified
2790: history, but they are not finite package coordinates for this proposition unless
2791: their contents are represented in \path{main.tex} or in the submitted support
2792: surface.  The TeX source fixes the displayed six-row ledger.  This state is denoted
2793: $P^{+}_{6}$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:nested-package-states}: it is the row-frozen
2794: certificate state for $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, before the submitted manual theorem-ledger
2795: coordinate $a_7$ is adjoined.  The review-bundle files in this definition are
2796: supplemental-record components: claims that depend on them are conditional on
2797: those files being submitted with matching paths and records, or on an archival
2798: equivalent satisfying Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}.
2799: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}
2800: and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} form a finite submission-local
2801: acceptance-entry ledger for the load-bearing current-package claims used in
2802: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-package-path-verified-instantiation}.  For each entry
2803: $a_i$, the two tables determine the certificate fields
2804: \[
2805: \mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)
2806: \]
2807: and each such certificate is locally checkable at the path-verified evidence
2808: level.  No load-bearing claim outside these six entries is accepted by the
2809: current-package instantiation.
2810: \end{proposition}
2811: 
2812: \begin{proof}
2813: The acceptance-entry ledger is finite because Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}
2814: has six rows.  Its rows enumerate exactly the assertions on which the current
2815: package corollary depends: the bounded imported source interface, the existence
2816: of the presentation-only package, and the four case-study lessons for intake
2817: isolation, overlap blocking, fake-extension rejection, and artifact limitation.
2818: The third column assigns the claim-kind set $K_i$ for each accepted assertion.
2819: These kinds all lie in the seven-kind domain of the dispatch function $\Gamma$.
2820: 
2821: For each row, Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} supplies the remaining
2822: certificate fields.  The declared evidence level $\ell_i$ is path-verified in
2823: every row.  The durable manuscript contribution is the finite row text printed in
2824: the tables and the proof that these fields have the required schema shape.  The
2825: matching assertions about JSON mirrors, pass-record files, manifest entries, and
2826: verifier logs are supplemental-record assertions and require the named
2827: \path{review_bundle/} files to be present.  The evidence surface $E_i$ is a finite
2828: list of submission-local files, review-bundle records, pinned source-interface
2829: records, or explicit historical paths.  The promotion field $H_i$ is either the current active-paper marker
2830: \path{main.tex} together with \path{PIPELINE.md}, or the explicit marker that the
2831: case row does not itself cross a new route boundary.  The pass-record field
2832: $R_i$ names the finite gate records used by the row: source-interface extraction
2833: and consistency for $a_1$, package and submission-pack records for $a_2$,
2834: promotion for $a_3$, overlap and submission-state for $a_4$, theorem-content and
2835: discovery gating for $a_5$, and artifact status plus limitation-ledger gating for
2836: $a_6$.
2837: 
2838: The discovery field behaves exactly as required by the certificate schema.  It is
2839: present only for $a_5$, the row whose accepted claim has discovery kind, and it
2840: names the pinned source-interface rows that supply the certificate-and-ledger,
2841: demotion, lineage, pipeline-safety, and main-pipeline witnesses.  For the other
2842: rows the claim-kind set does not contain discovery, so $D_i$ is absent.  Finally,
2843: the boundary field $B_i$ records the same limitation in every row: the package is
2844: using path-verified evidence, not command-run evidence, unless the command,
2845: source commit, environment, exit code, and log path are explicitly recorded.
2846: Consequently the ledger is historical and path-verified; it does not assert a
2847: fresh Lean build, daemon pass, full-source rerun, Rule110 or GroundCompiler
2848: rerun, artifact-suite validation, or external venue acceptance.  Thus each row
2849: determines a locally checkable publication certificate
2850: \(\mathcal C(a_i)\).
2851: 
2852: It remains only to see that the ledger is complete for the current-package
2853: instantiation.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-package-path-verified-instantiation}
2854: uses no unlisted positive claim: its source-interface premise is $a_1$, its
2855: package premise is $a_2$, and its four bounded case-study premises are
2856: $a_3$--$a_6$.  Any proposed stronger reading, such as a fresh full-source rebuild,
2857: publication-daemon run, Rule110 dynamic rerun, artifact-suite semantic
2858: validation, unledgered discovery claim, or external venue acceptance, has no
2859: acceptance entry in Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and also violates
2860: the boundary field in Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}.  By
2861: Theorems~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} and
2862: \ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}, such a reading is therefore
2863: rejected by a finite obstruction subrecord rather than accepted by this
2864: package.\end{proof}
