2866: \subsection{Current-package instantiation}
2867: 
2868: \noindent\emph{Six-row and seven-row states.}
2869: The current-package certificate state in this subsection is the six-row state
2870: $P^{+}_{6}$ with acceptance rows \path{a1}--\path{a6}.  The later
2871: theorem-obligation integration in
2872: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} is the seven-row extension
2873: $P^{+}_{7}$, obtained from $P^{+}_{6}$ by adding exactly the manual submitted
2874: theorem-ledger coordinate \path{a7}.  Thus the two theorems describe nested support states,
2875: not competing current-package mirrors.
2876: 
2877: \begin{theorem}[Current-package schema-relative finite-record certificate exactness]
2878: \label{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}
2879: For the six-row finite current-package state $P^{+}_{6}$ of
2880: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}, assume the
2881: support-surface availability condition of
2882: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}: the submitted supplemental
2883: material contains the exact \path{review_bundle/} directory with
2884: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, with refreshed digest rows and
2885: rerun finite-verifier logs for every named bundle coordinate regenerated after
2886: the digest surface was written.  With that support
2887: surface present, the six ledger entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ as printed in
2888: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
2889: and as checked by \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} are
2890: exactly the accepted schema-relative finite-record instantiation of the finite-record interface theorem,
2891: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  More precisely, the package map
2892: \[
2893: a_i\longmapsto \mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)
2894: \]
2895: is a bijection from the accepted current-package claim rows printed in this
2896: source to the locally checkable certificates accepted by the submitted schema
2897: verifier at the declared path-verified evidence level, relative only to the
2898: finite fields read by that schema.  The assigned-gate rows are sound only in the
2899: finite-record sense of
2900: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}: they certify assigned
2901: gate coverage and declared evidence pointers, while any stronger assertion of
2902: independent gate implementation soundness would require source paths, commands,
2903: source states, environments, exit codes, and log paths for those gates.  Without
2904: that support surface, the theorem retains only the printed source-level inventory
2905: of six intended certificate rows
2906: and not the machine-readable current-package acceptance claim.  The durable TeX source
2907: fixes the six row statements and their negative boundaries; the supplemental
2908: record supplies the machine-readable mirror and verifier log only if it travels
2909: with the submission or with an archival replacement satisfying
2910: the final-support closure condition just stated.  Every
2911: current-package reading requiring a seventh accepted row, a different claim kind,
2912: a stronger evidence level, an additional gate pass record, a fresh command-run
2913: tuple, or an external venue decision is outside this bijection and is rejected by
2914: the finite obstruction skeleton and, more sharply, by the positive extension-budget
2915: criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  The exactness
2916: is therefore row-frozen, finite-record, and verifier-log bounded: editing any of
2917: the six rows or any machine-readable mirror changes the finite package unless the
2918: certificate verifier is rerun and the displayed tables and log are updated
2919: together.
2920: \end{theorem}
2921: 
2922: \begin{proof}
2923: \emph{Construction from the displayed rows.}
2924: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger} constructs,
2925: for each row $a_i$ of Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}
2926: and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}, a certificate
2927: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\).  The row set is finite and
2928: has exactly six elements.  The construction is injective because the row
2929: identifier, accepted claim text, and evidence surface in
2930: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} are part of the displayed record data;
2931: two different rows cannot have the same accepted current-package claim row.  It
2932: is surjective onto the accepted current-package certificates because the same
2933: proposition states that no load-bearing claim outside these six entries is
2934: accepted by the current-package instantiation.
2935: 
2936: \emph{Support-surface instantiation.}
2937: Conditioned on the supplemental records being present, matching the displayed
2938: paths, and matching the final digest manifest
2939: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md},
2940: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} supplies the finite
2941: schema instance over \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
2942: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
2943: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
2944: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, the verifier script, and the
2945: recorded verifier log.  The recorded log states command
2946: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, source commit
2947: \path{automath repository commit 305ee93353b16babc461bfad1a7aee9c808ee268 plus final package digest manifest review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, final package digest manifest
2948: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, environment \path{Python 3.10.11 on Windows 10}, exit code \path{0}, and log path
2949: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  The companion logs
2950: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log} and
2951: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-05.log} likewise
2952: record exit code \path{0} against the same final digest manifest.  That instance checks kind-domain membership,
2953: path-verified evidence-level membership, assigned-gate coverage, the five
2954: pass-record fields, discovery-field discipline, and the path-verified command
2955: boundary for entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$.  Hence every certificate in the image of
2956: the displayed package map is accepted by the submitted schema verifier at exactly
2957: the declared path-verified level when that supplemental record surface is part of
2958: the review package.  The support surface is finite:
2959: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} later proves that the bundle-backed
2960: component for this package is admissible exactly when the exact
2961: \path{review_bundle/} directory is submitted with its final digest manifest.
2962: Without that finite surface,
2963: the TeX proof still supplies the finite schema classification of the displayed
2964: rows, but not the additional claim that the submitted executable mirror and log
2965: were available to the reviewer.  By
2966: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, the same verification
2967: does not audit the implementation internals of the named gates: it verifies the
2968: presence and local evidence surfaces of the assigned finite pass rows, and a
2969: fresh implementation-soundness reading would be a command-result escalation not
2970: present in these six certificates.
2971: 
2972: \emph{Maximality and non-escalation.}
2973: Conversely, a stronger reading changes at least one finite component of the
2974: record: it adds a row, changes $K_i$, changes $\ell_i$, adds a required gate in
2975: $R_i$, replaces the path-verified boundary $B_i$ by a command-run tuple, or adds
2976: an external venue-decision surface.  Such a record is not in the six-row image
2977: just identified.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, a
2978: genuine strengthening must pay a positive finite extension budget in added
2979: coordinates, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton},
2980: the first unpaid coordinate gives a bad subrecord: a missing evidence surface,
2981: missing gate record, missing promotion or venue field, missing discovery-source
2982: row, or unsupported command-run escalation.  Thus the six certificates are not a
2983: sample of an unrecorded full-pipeline execution; they are exactly the row-frozen,
2984: finite, path-verified package instantiation of
2985: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.\end{proof}
2986: 
2987: \begin{corollary}[Row-frozen current-package invariance]
2988: \label{cor:row-frozen-current-package-invariance}
2989: Under the support-surface hypotheses of
2990: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the current-package
2991: acceptance decision factors through the six displayed rows
2992: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\) and their certificate fields only.  Any two ambient repository,
2993: daemon, artifact, or venue states that induce the same six tuples
2994: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\), the same submitted support
2995: surface, and the same verifier log path induce the same accepted current-package
2996: certificate set.  Conversely, a reading that depends on a seventh row, a modified
2997: row, a fresh command execution, semantic validity of BEDC/Lean/Rule110 artifacts,
2998: implementation soundness of the verifier or gates, upload status, or venue
2999: decision is not invariant under this six-row projection and is accepted only
3000: after it is added as a finite extension coordinate and rechecked.  Thus the
3001: current-package theorem is exactly a row-frozen finite-record theorem, not a
3002: current-execution reproducibility theorem and not a semantic-validity theorem.
3003: \end{corollary}
3004: 
3005: \begin{proof}
3006: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} identifies the accepted
3007: current-package certificates with the bijective image of the six rows
3008: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\) under the map
3009: \(a_i\mapsto \mathcal C(a_i)\), conditioned on the submitted support surface and
3010: the recorded verifier log.  The verifier then reads only the finite fields of
3011: these tuples, the fixed dispatch table, and the cited submitted surfaces.  By
3012: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, two ambient states
3013: with identical values on those finite coordinates have the same verifier
3014: decision and the same finite certificate set.  This proves the stated invariance.
3015: 
3016: For the converse boundary, each listed stronger reading changes a coordinate not
3017: contained in the six-row projection: a seventh accepted row changes the row set;
3018: a modified row changes one of \(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i\); a fresh command
3019: execution changes the boundary field to command-run evidence; semantic validity
3020: or implementation soundness adds claim kinds, evidence surfaces, and gate rows;
3021: upload or venue status adds external route/submission fields.  By
3022: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, such a genuine
3023: strengthening has a positive finite extension budget, and by
3024: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} an unpaid coordinate is
3025: reported as a finite bad subrecord.  Hence the six-row exactness result cannot be
3026: read as a claim about fresh reproducibility or semantic validity; those readings
3027: require an enlarged record and a new acceptance check.\end{proof}
3028: 
3029: \begin{theorem}[Current-package semantic non-escalation]
3030: \label{thm:current-package-semantic-non-escalation}
3031: Let $P^{+}_{6}$ be the six-row current-package state of
3032: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, and assume the same
3033: support-surface availability condition.  For each accepted row $a_i$ with
3034: certificate
3035: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\), the following are
3036: equivalent for any proposed stronger reading $a_i^{+}$ of the same manuscript
3037: row.
3038: \begin{enumerate}
3039: \item $a_i^{+}$ is accepted by the finite-record safety interface for the current
3040: package.
3041: \item The package supplies a positive extension record
3042: \(\Delta(a_i,a_i^{+})\) of the normal form in
3043: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, and every nonempty
3044: coordinate of that record lies on the submitted support surface and passes its
3045: assigned gates.
3046: \end{enumerate}
3047: Consequently no row of $P^{+}_{6}$ entails, by itself, semantic correctness of
3048: BEDC source theory, Lean, Rule110/GroundCompiler, the certificate-verifier
3049: implementation, upload surfaces, or venue decisions.  In the current submission
3050: those stronger readings are certifiable only as extension claims; when the
3051: extension coordinates are absent they are rejected by a finite bad subrecord, and
3052: the accepted content remains exactly the six row-frozen finite-record certificates
3053: of Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.
3054: \end{theorem}
3055: 
3056: \begin{proof}
3057: Fix $i$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the row
3058: $a_i$ is accepted exactly through the finite tuple
3059: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\) at the declared
3060: path-verified level, and the theorem identifies no additional accepted
3061: current-package row outside the six-row image.  If $a_i^{+}$ is accepted as a
3062: properly stronger reading, then it is not record-equivalent to $a_i$ in the
3063: preorder of Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  Hence
3064: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} collects the finite
3065: differences into a positive extension record
3066: \(\Delta(a_i,a_i^{+})\) consisting of added claim kinds, evidence surfaces,
3067: gate-pass rows, boundary fields, discovery rows, or command-run data.  Acceptance
3068: forces each added coordinate to satisfy the corresponding schema clause A1--A6,
3069: so the extension coordinates must be submitted and gate-passing.  This proves
3070: (1)$\Rightarrow$(2).
3071: 
3072: Conversely, suppose the positive extension record is supplied on the submitted
3073: surface and every added coordinate passes its assigned gates.  Enlarging
3074: \(\mathcal C(a_i)\) by those coordinates gives the certificate tuple displayed in
3075: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  The six schema clauses of
3076: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} then hold for the
3077: enlarged reading, and Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} accepts
3078: $a_i^{+}$ exactly at the declared evidence level.  This proves (2)$\Rightarrow$(1).
3079: 
3080: The listed semantic readings are all proper strengthenings of a six-row finite
3081: certificate: BEDC and Lean semantic correctness add formal-source or
3082: implementation-soundness coordinates; Rule110/GroundCompiler semantic validity
3083: adds artifact-semantic or evaluator-run coordinates; verifier implementation
3084: soundness adds implementation source, command, and pass records; upload and venue
3085: readings add external support-surface or decision fields.  None of those
3086: coordinates is one of the six row-frozen certificates identified by
3087: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.  If the corresponding
3088: extension record is absent,
3089: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns the first
3090: missing evidence surface, gate record, external boundary, discovery row, or
3091: command-run tuple as a finite bad subrecord.  Therefore the current-package
3092: exactness theorem is a finite-record certificate theorem and not a semantic
3093: correctness theorem for the named systems or surfaces.\end{proof}
3094: 
3095: \begin{corollary}[Current package path-verified instantiation]
3096: \label{cor:current-package-path-verified-instantiation}
3097: For the current presentation package, let $P$ be the finite package consisting
3098: of the primary source \path{submission_abstract.tex}, this supplement
3099: \path{main.tex}, and the exact-or-archive-equivalent review-bundle support
3100: surface of Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, together
3101: with the executable certificate records named in
3102: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}.  Root-level workflow
3103: diagnostic files are local historical/path-verified context only unless their
3104: records are represented on that submitted surface.  The path catalogue
3105: in $P$, through the exact six-row certificate map of
3106: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, instantiates
3107: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, at the
3108: path-verified evidence level as follows.  The imported discovery-gate interface
3109: is fixed by the pinned newmath commit
3110: \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}, the source-interface table,
3111: the JSON mirror, and the bundled source snapshot; the four case-study records
3112: are bounded witnesses for promotion, route-overlap/submission-state,
3113: theorem-content, and artifact-limitation gates; and every such case record is
3114: usable only for the bounded manuscript lesson named in its row.  The finite
3115: acceptance entries and certificate fields are exactly the rows of
3116: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}.
3117: Therefore the
3118: current package supports exactly the path-verified publication-safety claim that
3119: its load-bearing case-study assertions are reviewable, gate-typed, and equipped
3120: with locally checkable publication certificates at the path-verified level.  The
3121: corollary is historical and record-level: it instantiates the certificate
3122: constructor using the listed paths, tables, and bundle records, not by rerunning
3123: the whole workflow.  It does not instantiate any stronger claim that the full
3124: newmath source tree, a publication daemon, an artifact validator, or the Rule110
3125: dynamic artifact suite was freshly rerun.
3126: \end{corollary}
3127: 
3128: \begin{proof}
3129: The package $P$ contains exactly the submission-local source files and manifest
3130: records named in the statement.  Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}
3131: shows that the source-interface table is the complete imported interface used by
3132: this paper, and Propositions~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface}
3133: and \ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} identify the same
3134: interface by pinned commit, JSON mirror, and bundled source snapshot.  Hence the
3135: discovery-gate hypotheses of the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} are
3136: not supplied by free prose; they are supplied, at the bounded source-interface
3137: level, by recorded paths and record files in $P$.  Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}
3138: adds the submission-local acceptance entries for these premises: the imported
3139: source-interface claim is entry $a_1$, and the route/submission package claim is
3140: entry $a_2$.
3141: 
3142: For the case-study claims, Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion} states that a
3143: record naming the source or artifact surface, failed invariant, gate decision,
3144: and safe manuscript lesson is a path-verified safety-interface witness and not a
3145: command-run witness unless the extra command-run fields are recorded.
3146: Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} applies this criterion to
3147: the four case kinds used in the paper: promotion, route overlap, theorem-content
3148: growth, and artifact limitation.  Thus the rows later displayed in
3149: Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}, together with the manifest file named in $P$,
3150: provide passable path-verified evidence surfaces for exactly those bounded case
3151: claims.  Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}
3152: identifies these four case-study claims with entries $a_3$--$a_6$ and records
3153: their fields $K$, $\ell$, $E$, $H$, $R$, $D$, and $B$.  Applying
3154: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, to these recorded surfaces gives
3155: the stated current-package instantiation: each bounded case-study assertion
3156: obtains a finite certificate whose fields lie in $P$, the review bundle, or the
3157: explicit historical-evidence boundary.  The obstruction
3158: skeleton theorem then shows what a reviewer would have to find to reject any
3159: purported stronger reading: a missing path, missing gate row, missing promotion
3160: record, missing discovery-source row, or unsupported command-run escalation.
3161: Since $P$ records no command, environment, exit code, and log path for a fresh
3162: full-source rebuild, publication-daemon execution, or Rule110 dynamic rerun, and
3163: since Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract} gives the verifier no
3164: output channel for fresh case-study rerun validity, the definition of command-run
3165: evidence prevents the corollary from asserting any of those stronger claims.\end{proof}
